 "draft": "NARRATOR: In this city there is a creature that cannot see anything at all because it has no eyes.\nNARRATOR: Instead it hunts by listening for the sound of people talking.\nCOP: Attention everyone, please remain calm and proceed to the shelters.\nNARRATOR: The creature attacked the police officer as soon as he spoke.\nNARRATOR: Theo was a boy who was famous at school for never being quiet.\nNARRATOR: He and his sister Ava hid behind a police car and tried not to make a sound.\nNARRATOR: A phone rang but the creature did not react, because it only hunts voices.\nNARRATOR: Theo had the idea to use the police radios to throw his voice.\nTHEO: Hey, over here!\nNARRATOR: It worked until the rain broke the radio.\nAVA: Theo, look out behind you!\nTHEO: No, come after me instead!\nNARRATOR: Theo ran into the subway and kept shouting, and the echoes confused the monster.\nNARRATOR: The monster dug into the street until it collapsed.\nAVA: You can talk now.\nTHEO: I think I'll stay quiet.",
 "critique": [
  "Opening sentence is 18 words \u2014 the hook must be two short punches: 'It has no eyes.' / 'It doesn't need them.'",
  "'Instead it hunts by listening' is soft. The rule needs to be 5 words and then PROVEN by a kill-shot cut mid-sentence.",
  "Show the cop getting cut off mid-word \u2014 the cut IS the rule. Don't narrate it afterwards.",
  "Theo's flaw is told. Plant it with a specific (report cards) and make his silence physical (hand over Ava's mouth, no line).",
  "Phone beat is good but needs thunder + siren too: 'Only voices' is the rule clarification and a mini-reveal.",
  "Radio plan must read in one line: 'He talked. Just not from where he was standing.'",
  "Reversal is cheap if it's random \u2014 the rain is set up from shot 1, so the rain killing the radio is earned.",
  "'I think I'll stay quiet' is a statement; 'I think I'm good' is funnier and more natural. Narrator button lands the irony.",
  "Need a silent beat: when the street collapses, drop the score completely."
 ],
